<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Lübeck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1629">1629</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Lübeck · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> of 1629 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a>'s intervention in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. Defeated by the imperial armies of Tilly and Wallenstein, which had occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, Christian recovered his lands without paying an indemnity but promised to stay out of the affairs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lubeck.jpg" alt="Treaty of Lübeck" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wallenstein et al. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titelblatt_Luebecker_Frieden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
